WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.871
What’s the next element?

00:00:03.871 --> 00:00:10.300
Hassium. I know nothing about hassium, shall
we make something up?

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:17.760
Hassium is an element which I knew nothing
about. But now I discover it’s much more

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:25.020
interesting than I thought. We’re here in
the German state of Hesse at the institute

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:34.780
GSI were hassium was first made in 1984. Hassia
is the Latin name for this part of Germany

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:43.350
Hesse and hence it is named after the place
it was discovered. So down here we have the

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:53.070
accelerator that accelerated atoms of iron
to bang into a lead target to make element

00:00:53.070 --> 00:01:01.300
108 Hassium. Element 108 is actually in the
same group of the periodic table as iron,

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:09.869
that much I knew when I first talked about
it. But what I didn’t realise is that the

00:01:09.869 --> 00:01:17.899
element has a few atoms that are long enough
lived that you can really do chemistry. So

00:01:17.899 --> 00:01:24.740
once the atom flies off from the back of the
target when it has been created you can pass

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:37.960
it through an atmosphere of oxygen and make
the oxide hassium tetroxide Hs04. And the

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:45.079
iron of this molecule, this oxide can then
be followed to see how volatile it is and

00:01:45.079 --> 00:01:54.249
compared with osmium tetroxide. Osmium tetroxide
is a white solid which is quite volatile,

00:01:54.249 --> 00:02:00.950
it easily evaporates and you can show that
hassium tetroxide does not evaporate quite

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:07.929
so easily. But it’s amazing this is an element
only forty atoms have been made but you can

00:02:07.929 --> 00:02:15.680
tell how its oxide behaves. Osmium has one
of the highest melting points of any element

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:21.540
that’s known. The people who make periodic
tables where you have a sample of each element

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:28.250
have great trouble melting osmium to make
a sample, so it may be that hassium would

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:33.840
have an even higher melting point, it could
be that it’s the highest melting point in

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:38.680
the periodic table. But one would have to
make a lot more atoms than that if you were

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:44.439
going to find out what the melting point was.
I think people may be able to calculate it,

00:02:44.439 --> 00:02:51.000
but the melting point depends on the strength
of the interaction between the atoms and unless

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:59.249
you have quite a few atoms that’s very difficult
to measure. And quite a few means many tens

00:02:59.249 --> 00:03:05.859
of millions because even a small particle,
a so called nanoparticle, of metal can have

00:03:05.859 --> 00:03:08.370
quite different properties from a large lump.

